# Citation metadata for this repository, in Citation File Format 1.2.0.
# https://citation-file-format.github.io
#
# GitHub renders this as the "Cite this repository" button. The fuller
# guidance, including how to cite an individual source held in the archive
# rather than the project as a whole, is published at https://cc-vuln.org/cite/
#
# Deliberately absent:
#
#   version / date-released  There are no releases yet, and a hand-written
#       version string here would be wrong the first time anyone forgot it.
#       The citable version identifier is the commit, published with every
#       build at https://cc-vuln.org/version.json
#   authors' personal names  The project is published pseudonymously and says
#       so on /about/. The author is the project, entered as an entity, so a
#       reference manager renders "cc-vuln.org" rather than inventing a
#       surname. Attribution to the project is what CC BY 4.0 requires.
#   doi  None minted. See docs/publication.md before minting one: a permanent
#       deposit of the whole repository would publish third-party captures
#       under an identifier designed to be hard to retract.

cff-version: 1.2.0
title: COLDCARD predictable-RNG incident archive
message: >-
  If you use this record, cite the original publisher of the material first and
  this archive second, as the preserved state and the evidence that the state
  existed. Guidance and worked examples: https://cc-vuln.org/cite/
type: dataset
abstract: >-
  A primary-source archive of the July 2026 COLDCARD predictable-RNG incident.
  It preserves what each party published and how it changed, organises the
  material, and explains it without adjudicating between the people involved.
  Registered sources are polled on a schedule; a snapshot is stored whenever the
  source's canonical comparison text changes, and every poll is recorded whether
  or not it changed anything, which is what lets a revision be bounded in time
  rather than merely observed. Every material claim on the published site
  carries an evidence basis (verified, reported, derived or unverified, with
  contested recorded separately where sources disagree) and links to the
  artefact behind it. The repository contains the captures, the capture and
  review tooling, the source registry, the classification of every detected
  difference, and the site that presents them.
authors:
  - name: cc-vuln.org
    website: https://cc-vuln.org
    email: info@cc-vuln.org
repository-code: https://github.com/cc-vuln/coldcard-rng-incident
url: https://cc-vuln.org
# Two licences, because the project can only license what it created: the
# project's own writing and data are CC BY 4.0, the tooling and site code are
# MIT. Third-party material under archive/ is neither, and remains its authors'
# copyright: see LICENSE-CONTENT.md section 3.
license:
  - CC-BY-4.0
  - MIT
keywords:
  - bitcoin
  - hardware wallet
  - random number generation
  - entropy
  - vulnerability disclosure
  - web archiving
  - primary sources
  - COLDCARD
  - Coinkite
